libopenwch — Bluetooth LE
=========================

What this is
------------

A thin layer over WCH's closed-source Bluetooth stack, for the CH582/CH583.
It renames the parts of WCH's API a peripheral needs into this project's
lowercase_snake_case style, documents them, and gets out of the way.  It is
not a protocol stack and it contains no Bluetooth logic.

The stack itself is `wch/LIBCH58xBLE.a`, vendored verbatim from WCH's CH583
EVT package and redistributed under their Apache-2.0 licence.  See
`wch/LICENSE` and the NOTICE file at the top of the tree.  **That directory is
not LGPL**; the wrapper around it is.

  wch/LIBCH58xBLE.a                    WCH's stack, unmodified
  ../include/libopenwch/ble/wch/       WCH's header, unmodified

Layout
------

  include/libopenwch/ble/common.h      shared types and status codes
  include/libopenwch/ble/tmos.h        the task/message scheduler
  include/libopenwch/ble/gap.h         advertising and the peripheral role
  include/libopenwch/ble/gatt.h        the GATT server
  include/libopenwch/ble/ble.h         start-up; includes the rest
  lib/ble/ble.c                        ble_init() and friends
  lib/ble/ble_tmos.c                   scheduler forwarders
  lib/ble/ble_gap.c                    GAP and peripheral-role forwarders
  lib/ble/ble_gatt.c                   GATT server forwarders

The wrapper objects are built into `libopenwch_ch5xx58x.a` because the stack
is CH58x-only.  They cost nothing to an application that never calls them: an
archive member is only pulled in to satisfy an undefined symbol, so a program
that does not use `ble_*` never references the vendor archive either.

Linking
-------

The stack is *not* part of `libopenwch_ch5xx58x.a`.  An application using this
layer must link, in this order:

  libopenwch_ch5xx58x.a     our drivers and the wrappers
  wch/LIBCH58xBLE.a         the stack the wrappers call
  libopenwch_mini_libc_*.a  the memcpy() the stack needs
  -lgcc

Applications built from `template/` get this from `LIBOPENWCH_BLE=1`.  Order
matters: the vendor archive must be scanned after ours (which introduces the
references) and before the libc (which supplies `memcpy`).

Memory
------

The stack allocates from a heap the application declares and passes in
`ble_config_t.heap`; there is no default.  6 KiB is what WCH's examples use
and 4 KiB is the floor.  Declare it with `BLE_HEAP_DEFINE`.

Some of the stack's own code is placed in `.highcode` and runs from RAM.  The
library's linker script already handles that: `.highcode` has a RAM VMA and a
flash LMA, and the start-up code copies it.  Nothing extra is needed.

Scope
-----

Present: TMOS, GAP parameters, the peripheral role, the GATT server, and
stack start-up.

Absent, and reachable only through WCH's own names in the vendor header:

  * central, observer and broadcaster roles
  * the bonding manager (GAPBondMgr_*), and with it persistent bonding, which
    also needs flash access
  * OTA update and the mesh stack
  * the attribute-table macros used to build a service

The MAC address is the application's to supply.  WCH's helper for reading the
address burned into the part is a macro over `libISP583.a`, another closed
binary this project does not vendor; see the flash gap described in
`status.md`.

Not wrapped on purpose
----------------------

`ble_gap_role_set_param()` takes a `void *` and a length, exactly as WCH's
does, because one call carries a one-byte flag, a two-byte interval and a
31-byte advertising payload, and the parameter id is what says which.  Typed
convenience wrappers would be a nicer API and are exactly the kind of
opinionated layer this wrapper exists to avoid; put them in the application.
